WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.400
If you have been following along with this&nbsp;
series of tutorials, you may have noticed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.800
that there is no Undo within Chimera, other than&nbsp;
the Undo Selection feature. In this final video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.800
we will explore three workarounds for this&nbsp;
issue, using the hemoglobin structure 4hhb.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.400
We can use the command line to save a&nbsp;
structure's position in the graphics window.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.840
Let's say you are ready to begin exploring this&nbsp;
structure but don't want to lose the current view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:43.040
The solution is to type into the command line&nbsp;
"savepos p1" and press Enter. This command defines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:50.240
the current position as "position 1" in Chimera's&nbsp;
memory. Now you can safely explore the structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.800
in the graphics window. To return to the original&nbsp;
position, all you need to do is enter into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:04.880
command line "reset p1". You can extend this basic&nbsp;
technique to save more than one position — just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:11.840
label your second position as "p2", your third&nbsp;
as "p3", and so on as you define more positions.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.000
Following the "savepos" command, you can also&nbsp;
type names that are more specific than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.160
p+integer naming system that I just demonstrated.&nbsp;
In fact, you can type whatever name you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.560
For example, we could save this position&nbsp;
using the command "savepos heme" since we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:37.440
are focusing in on a particular heme group.&nbsp;
The reset format is the same as before — we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.640
just enter into the command line "reset&nbsp;
heme" to recover the initial position.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.640
For this workaround, it is key that you are&nbsp;
working within a session file that has previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:02.480
been saved using Save As. Chimera's file directory&nbsp;
makes it easy to identify such files. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.240
list, files marked with an extension have been&nbsp;
saved beforehand, while those without an extension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.520
have not. With that knowledge, let's open the&nbsp;
hemoglobin structure we have been looking at.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:21.200
The idea behind this workaround is to save the&nbsp;
session as you modify the structure. Let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.840
we go to Actions, Ribbon, and turn on&nbsp;
Ribbon Edging; now we can go to File&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:37.120
and save the change. Then we decide to select the&nbsp;
heme groups and change their depiction to Sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.880
But at this point, you realize that you like the&nbsp;
heme groups better in their original depiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:52.960
So rather than saving this change, the next&nbsp;
step is to close the session. Upon reopening it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.400
the heme groups are restored, but the ribbon&nbsp;
edging remains because we had previously saved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:04.880
that change. In this way, we can effectively&nbsp;
create an artificial Undo within Chimera.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:18.400
Finally, we can use Chimera's animation interface&nbsp;
to store structure depiction modifications. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.320
making any changes, let's start by taking a&nbsp;
snapshot of the structure in its default state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:35.440
Now we go to Actions, Color, All Options, and&nbsp;
selectively color the ribbons cyan. Then we stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:45.840
and take a snapshot of the result. For our next&nbsp;
change, we will select the heme groups and color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:57.280
them red. We then take another snapshot of the&nbsp;
result. Next we select chain A, edge its ribbons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.840
and snapshot the result. Now we have created&nbsp;
a history of our depiction changes within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.840
animation interface. By left-clicking on&nbsp;
the snapshots, we can easily recover past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:20.000
states of the structure and access any&nbsp;
of the depictions created along the way.

